            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Aaron"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great recipe. I made pork shish kabobs using it. I did spice it up a bit and added some cayenne peppers for a little kick. Also reserved some for basting while on the grill. I will use this one again",
            "datePublished": "2021-05-17T12:10:35.950Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "MissBadger"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Super yummy!!! Didn't have any ginger, but did add just a pinch or two of red pepper flakes. Marinated pork chops for about 24 hrs, then grilled. Soooo amazing! The chops were tender, and the flavors were perfectly balanced. Keeper!",
            "datePublished": "2009-09-16T20:00:43.903Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Steve Perchalla"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I also doubled up on the wine and used honey instead of molasses it was awesome thank you!",
            "datePublished": "2019-11-23T22:41:26.747Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Marianne"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We had this tonight, marinated for only 1 1/2 hours with Costco's pork brisket grillers (really a nice cut). It was kind of a last minute idea, but it was really worth the try. I made the marinade exactly as stated, and I grilled the pork for only about 15 minutes on indirect heat. We'll do this one again.",
            "datePublished": "2009-07-20T20:48:37.830Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Meems"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Perfect as is! Made the best kabobs. Chopped 2+ pounds of nice sirloin, red/green peppers, onions, and marinated overnight. Then I skewered it all with some baby portabellas and put them on the grill. Great served with some rice and red wine!",
            "datePublished": "2010-05-16T19:39:29.390Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Corie"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We used this marinade on a pork tenderloin this evening and we just loved it! I usually don't care for marinades that are heavy on the soy sauce, but I was impressed how well it turned out. We used low sodium soy sauce and a lighter shiraz, which helped and the molasses flavor is subtle and really, really good. Brown sugar would have been great here too. Just that little bit of sweetness from the molasses added a nice crust from the grill on the outside of the meat and it was tender and perfect on the inside. We also did another tenderloin with straight bbq sauce just in case we didn't like this marinade, and there was a ton of the bbq tenderloin left over and none of this one!!! A sign of a great recipe!",
            "datePublished": "2008-07-12T19:06:45.627Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Alison"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used equal parts soy sauce (lite) and old Shiraz. This is a tasty way to used wine that is past it's drinking prime.",
            "datePublished": "2015-03-02T15:02:37.500Z"
          },
